node_modules/
dist/
.env
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.log
.DS_Store
runs/
# The same path again without the slash, because on the lab container `runs` is a SYMLINK onto a
# mounted volume (/srv/a11y-runs) so the corpus survives a container rebuild -- and git's trailing
# slash means "directory", which a symlink is not. So `runs/` matches on a developer's Mac, where it
# is a real directory, and silently does not match on the box that actually holds the corpus: it
# showed as untracked in every `git status` there, one `git add -A` away from committing a symlink
# pointing at a path that exists on exactly one machine.
/runs
# Downloaded encoders and scratch artefacts are not ours to version. The TRAINED SCORER is: it is 27 KB,
# it is this project's actual output, and without it in the repo the LLM-free judge cannot run for anyone
# but the machine that trained it — which is exactly how a shipped Action would have had no model at all.
# The training report ships with it as provenance: which corpus, which encoder hash, which thresholds.
# Note the shape: `models/*` and not `models/`. Git does not descend into an EXCLUDED DIRECTORY, so a
# negation under `models/` can never fire and the files could only be added with `git add -f` — which
# means a future retrain would silently not be committed. Each level has to be re-included in turn.
# The weights now live in `@a11y-witness/scorer` (PLAN.md M3), so only the ENCODER is ignored — the 27 KB of
# heads and the training report are tracked, and nothing under `packages/scorer/models` needs re-including
# level by level because no ancestor is excluded any more. The lesson above still applies to `models/*`
# below, which is kept because a retrain writes its intermediate output there.
packages/scorer/models/encoders/

# `npm pack` includes UNTRACKED files, so the encoder sitting inside the package directory would be shipped
# to every consumer if `packages/scorer/package.json` listed `models` instead of `models/screenreader-scorer`.
# That is why the allow-list names the subdirectory.

models/*

# Corpus snapshots (scripts/corpus-snapshot.mjs) — local archives, never committed
backups/

# tsc --build output from the project-reference fixtures (src/packaging/project-references.test.ts)
scripts/tsconfig-fixtures/*/dist/
scripts/tsconfig-fixtures/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Package build output (npm run build)
packages/*/dist/
packages/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# OUR tailnet, not the project's. This repo is generic; the Tailscale policy below is one deployment's
# access rules and one home network's addresses, and neither belongs to anyone else using this tool.
# `tailscale.yml` is kept because it derives everything from `inventory.yml` and so works for any fleet —
# the POLICY is what is specific, not the mechanism.
packages/worker-fleet/tailscale-acl.hujson

# Hosts that are ours and are not capture infrastructure. `tailscale.yml` merges this with the inventory
# when it exists, so a personal machine can be reachable over the tailnet without being published in a
# public repo as though it were part of the fleet.
packages/worker-fleet/ansible/*.local.yml

# Whatever agent tooling the person at the keyboard happens to use.
#
# `.agents/` and `.clinerules/` are per-operator assistant configuration, and `skills-lock.json` pins
# third-party skills from someone else's account. None of it is this project: a stranger cloning the repo
# would inherit one contributor's editor setup as though it were a build requirement. Same reasoning as
# the Tailscale policy above — the mechanism can be shared, one person's configuration cannot.
#
# Ignored rather than deleted, because they are useful locally and the point is only to keep them out of
# a public, generic repo.
.agents/
.clinerules/
skills-lock.json

# Coverage output. Regenerated by the test run, never reviewed from the tree.
coverage/
